---
import PageLayout from "../../layouts/default-no-header.astro";
const title =
	"Speculative Design | Halmstad Z | Portfolio of Manaj Chakrobarti";
const bodyClass = "works editorial";
import "../../styles/speculative-design.scss";
---

<PageLayout title={title} bodyClass={bodyClass}>
	<main class="editorial-page">
		<!-- Full bleed hero: image left, dark panel right -->
		<div class="hero">
			<div class="hero-image">
				<img
					src="/images/works/speculative-design/featured.jpg"
					alt="Halmstad Z card game"
				/>
				<span class="hero-image-credit">
					Photo by <span>Jay</span>
				</span>
			</div>

			<div class="content">
				<h1 class="hero-title">Halmstad <span>Z</span></h1>
				<p class="subhead">
					A fun card game where players work together to save the
					station city of Halmstad from a zombie attack.
				</p>

				<div class="hero-meta">
					<p>
						My teammate and I developed a cooperative card game. It
						is for a speculative design project situated in
						Stations­staden, “the station city” of Halmstad,
						motivated by a participatory economic world.
						Participatory economy, or parecon, is an economic model
						developed by economists Michael Albert and Robin Hahnel.
						Participatory planning is a part of Parecon and is used
						to allocate resources between consumers and workers.
					</p>
					<p>
						We created this game as a design artifact, interpreting
						participatory planning as play mechanics. The players
						collectively negotiate and prioritize missions in an
						alternative economic setting. While keeping in mind that
						each mission will affect three of the four core needs of
						the game. Each decision can affect needs both positively
						and negatively, and they have to keep those needs in
						balance.
					</p>
					<p>
						We also intended this game as a facilitation tool for
						budgeting, while keeping social values in mind. However,
						we haven’t explored those versions yet. We will work on
						that in the future.
					</p>
					<p>
						Finally, it uses a zombie-attack scenario in
						Stationsstaden to remove money and market-based
						incentives from the gameplay and add a fun mechanism.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="hero-data">
					<div class="hero-data-item">
						<span class="hero-data-label">Target audience</span>
						<span class="hero-data-value">Adults</span>
					</div>

					<div class="hero-data-item">
						<span class="hero-data-label">Design goals</span>
						<span class="hero-data-value">
							Collaborative, fun, and engaging.
						</span>
					</div>

					<div class="hero-data-item">
						<span class="hero-data-label">Design focus</span>
						<span class="hero-data-value">
							Game Design, Speculative Design, Participatory
							economy
						</span>
					</div>

					<div class="hero-data-item">
						<span class="hero-data-label">Team</span>
						<span class="hero-data-value">Manaj, Dylan</span>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<!-- Article content: sits in the main column -->
		<article class="article">
			<!-- Sticky sidenav: sits in the left wide zone -->
			<nav class="article-navigation">
				<ul class="no-list">
					<li class="no-top-space"><a href="#">Back to top</a></li>
					<li>
						<a href="#background">Background &amp; Challenge</a>
					</li>
					<li><a href="#teams">Team &amp; Responsibilities</a></li>
					<li><a href="#research">Research &amp; Discovery</a></li>
					<li><a href="#concept">Concept Development</a></li>
					<li><a href="#process">Design Process</a></li>
					<li class="no-bottom-space">
						<a href="#reflection">Reflection</a>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</nav>

			<h2 id="background">Research</h2>
			<p>
				Our research includes interviewing current passengers at the
				stations to better understand the problems they face. Observing
				in the Stationsstaden area to list the facilities. Using Google
				reviews to collect reviews on this and other nearby stations,
				and finally going through the planning documents of
				Stationsstaden.
			</p>
			<p>
				In our interviews in the station area, we found that people's
				needs are basic, such as better food options, free toilets,
				another overbridge, and a closer connection to the bus. Someone
				told us during the interview that they missed the train because
				there was no overbridge linked to the platform. So later I
				created a mission card to build an overbridge from the bus
				terminal to the platform.
			</p>

			<figure class="small center">
				<img
					src="/images/works/speculative-design/research-card.png"
					alt="One card related to research"
				/>
				<figcaption>
					Research findings helped create the mission cards.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				We found similar issues in Google reviews. The passengers want
				the waiting room to be kept open during cold nights, want more
				food options, and some people think this is enough. But in
				general, it was always basic needs in the station.
			</p>
			<div class="image-pair">
				<figure>
					<img
						src="/images/works/speculative-design/review-negative.png"
						alt="Nagative Review"
					/>
					<figcaption>From Google Reviews</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure>
					<img
						src="/images/works/speculative-design/review-positive.png"
						alt="Positive Review"
					/>
					<figcaption>From Google Reviews</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>

			<p>
				We used the data we gathered from reviews, observations we made
				at the station, and the Stationsstaden planning website to
				create the game missions. Like “Fix the bicycle path around the
				wall in the station”. We thought about a zombie attack scenario
				and how it would affect day-to-day activities, and we narrated
				accordingly.
			</p>

			<p>
				These data also led us to the basic needs. After trying
				different values across a couple of versions of the game, we
				extracted values from some missions. Because these values
				overlapped, choosing a negative one was difficult. We did this
				three times, and every time we found it hard to use negative
				values. That's why, in the end, we decided to use <a
					target="_blank"
					href="https://www.ucl.ac.uk/bartlett/sites/bartlett/files/universal_basic_services_-_the_institute_for_global_prosperity_.pdf"
					>universal basic needs
				</a>
				as a base for needs. These are Health care, Education, Legal and democracy,
				Shelter, Food, Transport, and information. We merged them into 4,
				as per the following table.
			</p>
			<figure>
				<img
					src="/images/works/speculative-design/table.png"
					alt="UCL values and our values"
				/>
				<figcaption>UCL values and our values</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<h2 id="research">Research</h2>

			<h3>Iterations</h3>

			<p>
				We have iterated on the game quite a couple of times, including
				3 major versions. Because at first we wanted to work with the
				value Justice. So we decided to choose between an individual
				train seat and a station upgrade. But in that version, when we
				tested with other people, we found that they always preferred
				helping a group over helping an individual. Because this way
				they can help more people in the game.
			</p>

			<p>
				We also had another version that wasn't based on a zombie
				attack. It was just about choosing an upgrade for the station.
				This narrative didn’t have the urgency in the storytelling or
				life threats to act fast. It was just an upgrade-a-station game.
				Back then, we didn’t have the meeples to make the gameplay
				strong, so the game was only updating the scoreboard, which was
				boring in my opinion.
			</p>
			<figure>
				<img
					src="/images/works/speculative-design/second_version.JPG"
					alt="Second version of the game"
				/>
				<figcaption>Second version of the game</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<h3>Testing</h3>

			<p>
				We tested three major versions of the game with people from
				other teams in the class.
			</p>
			<p>
				The first version didn’t work because it had two different types
				of choices, as I mentioned in the iterations section. But there
				were some helpful cards that explained the scenario, which
				didn’t always align with the choices. We kept those scenarios,
				and later used them as to-dos.
			</p>
			<p>
				The second version also had many smaller versions, but
				essentially it had a scoreboard to maintain the balance. And the
				task/mission had values on the back side, like this version.
				This was well received among peers, but we received feedback
				that a budget might help make this a facilitation tool for city
				planners. We tried to add budget to this version, using money
				and space as budget items. We failed. But we succeeded with
				using people as budget. Like a monetary budget, the number of
				people decreased. And later, we added the zombie narrative from
				one of our very early versions.
			</p>

			<h3>Speculative design studies</h3>
			<p>
				Speculative design studies include an understanding of
				speculative and critical design. Each student found some sample
				artifacts online, and we discussed them as a group. During this
				process, I came to understand that the purpose of critical
				design is to engage audiences and prompt them to reflect on the
				design's subject. This also led us to look into creating
				artifacts rather than a game as a speculative design project. We
				kept trying that for some weeks, and during the game-making
				process as a prototype, we realized that a game could be an
				artifact as well.
			</p>

			<h3>Role Play in Participatory Economy Value Fiction</h3>
			<p>
				We participated in a role-play seminar, where all of us in our
				class participated in a fictional play. We played different
				roles in a parecon society, where there are no paying clients,
				economic life is some form of collective, and every proposal
				disclosed its benefits and resource use. Then we played through
				different scenarios. Like a station waiting room, or shared
				mobility desk. This taught us to think about a differently
				valued society and how it may work. Also, it helped us give the
				role of council to the players.
			</p>

			<h3>Game making as prototype</h3>
			<p>
				We made the game a speculative design artifact because a game
				invites people to play and reflect on the situation without
				discussing it. They can continue thinking about future scenarios
				and communicate and solve problems together in a non-capitalist
				society. Without the game, the shift in thinking is hard to
				create.
			</p>

			<p>
				Games give us the option to narrate a worldview we want players
				to inhabit. We let them figure out the problem rather than us
				designers solving it for them.
			</p>

			<p>
				Also, it gives us a way to show the conflict without putting
				them in real danger, in this instance, in a zombie environment.
				While they are safe, the game's outcomes can vary from player to
				player. It could be an outcome we never expected.
			</p>

			<h3>Impact Vision and Design Fiction</h3>
			<p>
				We also participated in two design workshops, where we wrote
				stories about scenarios. Later, we all participated in a model
				world-making activity in which our scenarios came to life.
			</p>
			<figure class="wide">
				<img
					src="/images/works/speculative-design/research.png"
					alt="Research synthesis"
				/>
				<figcaption>Research Synthesis</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<p>
				Later, we created design fiction, in which we were given a
				future story and had to think it through and identify frictions
				and missing parts in the design.
			</p>
			<p>
				These two exercises taught me that people find unexpected things
				during testing. We found that in our test as well, people found
				problems with the old version. There were many questions about
				the gameplay. Because we haven’t written it yet.
			</p>

			<h2>The Game</h2>
			<p>
				The game is set in a future timeline when zombies attacked the
				city of Halmstad. All of its people fled, except for a few who
				took refuge in Stationsstaden and are trying to survive.
				Players, as council members, must prioritize missions that help
				these people survive while zombies roam outside the station. In
				the game, these people are represented by the meeples, and each
				game starts with 10 Meeples.
			</p>
			<p>
				Any number of players can play the game. The game starts with
				someone shuffling the mission cards and placing the top three
				cards face up on the game board. The group considers how each
				mission could affect their needs, and selects one mission.
			</p>
			<p>
				Once the group selects a mission to continue, they turn to the
				other side of the card, where there are two positive values and
				one negative value. The group then adjusts the scoreboard by
				moving the buttons to the right or left. The scoreboard is a
				7-column board, where points could range from -3 to +3. Each row
				represents a need, and there are 4 needs in total (Health,
				Information, Shelter, and Transport). A big part of the game is
				keeping these needs in balance, which means staying between -3
				and +3.
			</p>

			<p>
				Sometimes the selected mission card has a special instruction on
				the back. After updating the scoreboard, the group updates
				those, e.g., moves meeples or keeps the card next to the
				scoreboard and waits for any event, like scoring the lowest.
				Then that card might save them and keep the game going.
			</p>

			<p>
				After a couple of rounds, if selecting cards makes them go all
				the way to +3 in any specific category, for example, health,
				then the group receives the special card. It normally adds a
				bonus, and sometimes it can help to lose as well. For example,
				since they are doing very well in terms of health, 4 new people
				came here for medical purposes, which would increase the number
				of people at the station, which might not be so good. Anyway,
				once the group does what the card says, the button resets to 0
				on the scoreboard.
			</p>

			<p>
				The game loosely follows Parecon's planning meeting, where
				council and worker members decide on production priorities for
				the following year. But in gameplay, the group decides on
				missions monthly, so we can have 12 rounds over the year. Each
				game also finishes in 12 rounds. This totals 36 mission cards.
				The game also includes 16 special cards, with 4 for each need.
			</p>

			<p>
				The game ends if a counter moves to the leftmost square labeled
				"game over," outside the balanced need. Another way to lose is
				if the game's population falls below 4 or exceeds 12
				(represented by meeples). Because then it's not sustainable in
				the station.
			</p>

			<p>
				And winning is surviving for twelve rounds, keeping the needs in
				balance.
			</p>

			<h2>Connection to Participatory Economy</h2>
			<p>
				As we already know, the gameplay is inspired by the
				participatory planning process described on the parecon website:
				“Before the start of every new year, every workplace, household
				and federation council participate in an annual planning
				procedure...”. And the planning procedure occurs over a series
				of rounds. We were inspired by this, and it sets up our twelve
				rounds in the game. We just chose yearly planning to support our
				game. A shorter timeframe invites conversations among the
				players.
			</p>

			<p>
				We also chose a value to uphold, as Parecon is based on 6
				values. We chose efficiency among those. According to the
				Parecon website, efficiency is “To use our scarce resources and
				human talents wherever they return the greatest increase in
				human well-being.” We were motivated by the scarce-resource
				aspect, which is why the players have to choose one mission from
				three based on the scoreboard's needs: Health, Information,
				Shelter, and Transport. Otherwise, if resources were available,
				they could choose all the missions.
			</p>

			<p>
				However, we haven’t specified in our backstory that the game
				world's society is a participatory economy. But we made it so
				that when prioritizing missions, players don’t have to think
				about capitalist concepts like return on investment and mission
				costs. While we cannot think of any scenario in which we don't
				think about money, removing this capitalist view and that way of
				thinking would bring it closer to Parecon.
			</p>

			<p>
				So, finally, we can argue that using ideas from participatory
				planning, keeping resources in balance, removing the need for
				capital, and introducing a zombie-attack scenario makes it a
				speculative design project that uses Stationsstaden as a base.
			</p>

			<h2>Reflection</h2>

			<p>
				This project changed my view on speculative design. My
				understanding was that speculative design involves creating
				designs for the future or other critical designs that invite
				conversation. But after going through this project, my view of
				the artifact changed. Because a game can provide a way to create
				an alternative universe that I never thought of before.
			</p>

			<p>
				We wanted to make it a facilitation tool because working in
				corporate made me think of games as mostly a facilitation tool.
				However, to become a speculative project, this was not
				important. Also, to make it a successful facilitation tool, it
				needs more work. We will have to create systems that would let
				people make their own version of the game, which we had in mind
				but never managed to build.
			</p>

			<p>
				There was a breakthrough moment when I finished writing all the
				missions myself. Before that, we were talking a lot about game
				mechanics and how they weren't fitting, but the moment we had
				the narration, the mechanics fit right into place. Understanding
				that narration and creating stories are an important part of
				game development, which we read about, but seeing this in
				practice for the first time.
			</p>

			<p>
				The project management aspect was also not very good. There were
				three weeks when we kept circling back to the same discussion.
				We changed the topic, the story, and the values. But any values
				should work with this game, which we didn’t understand back
				then. We underestimated the power of narration in the gameplay.
			</p>

			<p>
				One thing that bothers me is that the project is not inside a
				participatory economy. We could easily make it into one. We
				haven’t gone that route because none of us are economists, and
				without one on the team, it's not a good idea to make a game. At
				least that's what I got from Reddit game discussion boards. We
				used some features of that economy mainly to prioritize and keep
				needs in balance. But we should’ve used more.
			</p>

			<p>
				While we were happy to find out from testing with different
				people that the game is fun and enjoyable, and I saw them decide
				collaboratively, there is still room for improvement, like the
				number: should we do 4 to 12, because this range is too small
				for a place like Stationsstaden. Maybe we can make it 40-120 in
				that case. And each meeple on the board will represent 10
				people. So we will keep working on these issues and keep
				updating the game.
			</p>
			<hr class="divider" />
			<p>Thank you for reading.</p>
		</article>
	</main>
</PageLayout>

<script>
	// Show the sidenav once the hero leaves the viewport
	const nav = document.querySelector(".article-navigation");
	const hero = document.querySelector(".hero");

	if (nav && hero) {
		const navObserver = new IntersectionObserver(
			([entry]) => {
				nav.classList.toggle("is-visible", !entry.isIntersecting);
			},
			{ threshold: 0 },
		);

		navObserver.observe(hero);
	}
</script>

<script>
	// Fade figures in as they scroll into view
	const figures = document.querySelectorAll(".article figure");

	const figureObserver = new IntersectionObserver(
		(entries) => {
			entries.forEach((entry) => {
				if (entry.isIntersecting) {
					entry.target.classList.add("is-visible");
					figureObserver.unobserve(entry.target);
				}
			});
		},
		{ threshold: 0.1 },
	);

	figures.forEach((el) => figureObserver.observe(el));
</script>
